1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,060
You and your crush are stuck in a time loop.

2
00:00:02,480 --> 00:00:05,040
Every morning at 7, the same day starts again.

3
00:00:05,560 --> 00:00:06,080
That's Leah.

4
00:00:06,540 --> 00:00:08,690
You've liked her for three years.

5
00:00:08,740 --> 00:00:12,200
Today is the last day of your senior year of college, and tonight is the

6
00:00:12,200 --> 00:00:13,060
graduation party.

7
00:00:13,540 --> 00:00:15,900
After that, she moves to the other side of the country.

8
00:00:16,820 --> 00:00:18,340
You were going to tell her today.

9
00:00:19,020 --> 00:00:22,620
You didn't. And then the day starts again.

10
00:00:22,670 --> 00:00:26,530
By the end of this video, you'll find out why Leah already knows your coffee

11
00:00:26,530 --> 00:00:31,550
order, what happens on loop 1,006, and the one thing that finally breaks the

12
00:00:31,550 --> 00:00:31,790
loop.

13
00:00:31,790 --> 00:00:34,250
On loop 2, you think it was a dream.

14
00:00:34,750 --> 00:00:37,930
Then your roommate Sam says the exact same thing he said yesterday.

15
00:00:38,470 --> 00:00:42,750
Bro. Last day. Last party. Last chance. Tell her, or I will.

16
00:00:43,330 --> 00:00:45,950
Same hoodie. Same cereal on the same hoodie.

17
00:00:46,390 --> 00:00:50,730
At the campus café, the same song comes on the radio at exactly 9:14.

18
00:00:51,310 --> 00:00:52,110
Remember that song.

19
00:00:52,650 --> 00:00:53,910
Leah is in line ahead of you.

20
00:00:54,230 --> 00:00:56,270
On loop 1, you didn't notice what she said.

21
00:00:56,690 --> 00:00:57,930
On loop 2, you do.

22
00:00:58,430 --> 00:01:00,970
Oat latte, extra hot, no foam. Right?

23
00:01:00,970 --> 00:01:02,370
You haven't ordered yet.

24
00:01:02,710 --> 00:01:03,650
That is your order.

25
00:01:04,050 --> 00:01:05,390
You've never told her your order.

26
00:01:06,230 --> 00:01:09,180
Loops 3, 4 and 5 go badly.

27
00:01:09,230 --> 00:01:10,090
You trip into her.

28
00:01:10,250 --> 00:01:11,090
You spill her coffee.

29
00:01:11,450 --> 00:01:13,970
You get hit in the face by a frisbee while saying, Hey, Leah.

30
00:01:14,550 --> 00:01:16,550
On loop 5, you tell Sam everything.

31
00:01:17,270 --> 00:01:18,250
I'm stuck in a time loop.

32
00:01:18,510 --> 00:01:19,410
Today keeps happening.

33
00:01:19,830 --> 00:01:20,930
You've said last chance,

34
00:01:21,190 --> 00:01:23,190
tell her or I will to me five times.

35
00:01:23,550 --> 00:01:24,110
Prove it.

36
00:01:24,490 --> 00:01:26,850
In 10 seconds, you're going to spill cereal on your hoodie.

37
00:01:27,650 --> 00:01:29,770
Okay, says Sam, covered in cereal.

38
00:01:29,770 --> 00:01:31,530
Okay. That's creepy.

39
00:01:31,910 --> 00:01:35,030
But also, if the day resets, there are zero consequences.

40
00:01:35,590 --> 00:01:39,050
So for the next three loops, you let Sam in on it, and he spends them eating an

41
00:01:39,050 --> 00:01:43,110
entire cake, riding a shopping cart down the library hill, and dyeing his hair

42
00:01:43,110 --> 00:01:43,470
green.

43
00:01:43,870 --> 00:01:47,530
Every morning he wakes up with no memory of it, and you have to explain it all

44
00:01:47,530 --> 00:01:48,110
over again.

45
00:01:48,550 --> 00:01:50,210
On loop 9, you try to escape.

46
00:01:50,610 --> 00:01:52,790
You get on a bus to a city four hours away.

47
00:01:53,150 --> 00:01:55,630
If the loop is tied to campus, maybe you can outrun it.

48
00:01:55,990 --> 00:02:00,350
At midnight, on a highway in the middle of nowhere, there's a white flash. And

49
00:02:00,350 --> 00:02:01,330
you wake up in your dorm.

50
00:02:01,870 --> 00:02:03,650
Loop 10. You can't outrun it.

51
00:02:04,030 --> 00:02:05,870
It's not the place. It's the day.

52
00:02:06,370 --> 00:02:08,890
So if you can't escape the day, you'll have to win it.

53
00:02:09,230 --> 00:02:10,730
On loop 11, you get organized.

54
00:02:11,250 --> 00:02:11,890
Here's what you know.

55
00:02:12,290 --> 00:02:14,190
The day always resets at seven.

56
00:02:14,530 --> 00:02:19,430
Nothing you do carries over except your memory. And you get unlimited practice.

57
00:02:19,950 --> 00:02:20,810
So you study.

58
00:02:21,230 --> 00:02:25,530
Her favorite band. Her favorite book. The fact that she hates mushrooms, which

59
00:02:25,530 --> 00:02:27,810
you learn by giving her mushrooms on loop 12.

60
00:02:27,810 --> 00:02:30,950
By loop 19, you've written the perfect speech.

61
00:02:31,510 --> 00:02:36,820
Leah. Since the day we met in freshman chemistry, I've... That's... really nice.

62
00:02:36,870 --> 00:02:37,770
Did you memorize that?

63
00:02:38,190 --> 00:02:39,290
She can tell.

64
00:02:39,870 --> 00:02:42,430
It sounds rehearsed, because it is.

65
00:02:42,990 --> 00:02:46,010
You try being funny. Being mysterious. Being casual.

66
00:02:46,410 --> 00:02:50,590
You try 50 different versions of yourself, and each one gets the same polite

67
00:02:50,590 --> 00:02:51,010
smile.

68
00:02:51,490 --> 00:02:53,230
Loop 27 is mysterious.

69
00:02:53,910 --> 00:02:56,790
You wear sunglasses indoors and say almost nothing.

70
00:02:56,790 --> 00:02:59,830
Leah asks if you're okay and offers you a glass of water.

71
00:03:00,410 --> 00:03:02,570
Loop 33 is musical.

72
00:03:03,070 --> 00:03:04,070
You can't play guitar yet.

73
00:03:04,410 --> 00:03:07,650
You play one chord, very confidently, for four minutes.

74
00:03:08,230 --> 00:03:09,690
Loop 41 is fate.

75
00:03:10,110 --> 00:03:13,530
You accidentally bump into her in five different places in one day.

76
00:03:13,850 --> 00:03:15,990
By the fifth one, she looks genuinely worried.

77
00:03:16,410 --> 00:03:17,810
Are you... following me?

78
00:03:18,170 --> 00:03:19,690
No. The universe is.

79
00:03:20,070 --> 00:03:21,450
You do not say that again.

80
00:03:21,910 --> 00:03:24,490
But on loop 50, you notice something you'd missed.

81
00:03:24,490 --> 00:03:27,750
At sunset, Leah sits alone on a bench in the quad.

82
00:03:28,170 --> 00:03:29,170
She doesn't look at her phone.

83
00:03:29,570 --> 00:03:32,350
She just stares at the sky, like she's waiting for something.

84
00:03:32,810 --> 00:03:35,530
Every loop. Same bench. Same face.

85
00:03:36,070 --> 00:03:36,810
Like she's tired.

86
00:03:37,330 --> 00:03:41,330
You don't know it yet, but that bench is the most important place in this whole

87
00:03:41,330 --> 00:03:41,810
story.

88
00:03:42,270 --> 00:03:46,350
On loop 51, you stop trying to win her over, and you just live the day.

89
00:03:46,730 --> 00:03:49,050
With unlimited Tuesdays, you get good at things.

90
00:03:49,410 --> 00:03:50,050
You learn guitar.

91
00:03:50,490 --> 00:03:52,230
You learn to cook something that isn't toast.

92
00:03:52,230 --> 00:03:56,210
You start learning Japanese from a library book, one day at a time.

93
00:03:56,550 --> 00:03:57,330
Here's a real number.

94
00:03:57,710 --> 00:03:58,110
The US

95
00:03:58,130 --> 00:04:02,790
Foreign Service Institute estimates it takes about 88 weeks of full-time study,

96
00:04:02,790 --> 00:04:07,630
around 2,200 classroom hours, for an English speaker to reach working fluency in

97
00:04:07,630 --> 00:04:08,030
Japanese.

98
00:04:08,570 --> 00:04:11,170
In a time loop, that's just a lot of Tuesdays.

99
00:04:11,470 --> 00:04:12,850
You start noticing other people.

100
00:04:13,230 --> 00:04:15,310
The guy whose bike chain snaps at 11:02.

101
00:04:15,550 --> 00:04:17,650
The woman who drops her phone at 3:40.

102
00:04:18,050 --> 00:04:18,850
You fix the bike.

103
00:04:19,210 --> 00:04:20,110
You return the phone.

104
00:04:20,450 --> 00:04:21,670
Every single loop.

105
00:04:21,670 --> 00:04:26,150
On loop 300, you stop trying to impress her, and you just sit on the bench at

106
00:04:26,150 --> 00:04:26,490
sunset.

107
00:04:26,930 --> 00:04:27,530
Not too close.

108
00:04:28,010 --> 00:04:29,110
You don't say anything clever.

109
00:04:29,590 --> 00:04:31,110
You say, Long day, huh?

110
00:04:31,570 --> 00:04:33,350
You have no idea, she says.

111
00:04:33,790 --> 00:04:37,230
And that's the most real thing she's said to you in 300 loops.

112
00:04:37,590 --> 00:04:38,250
So you go back.

113
00:04:38,890 --> 00:04:39,570
Every loop.

114
00:04:40,130 --> 00:04:41,570
Some days you talk for an hour.

115
00:04:41,990 --> 00:04:43,590
Some days you just watch the sky.

116
00:04:44,250 --> 00:04:47,530
She never mentions the day before, so you assume she can't remember.

117
00:04:47,990 --> 00:04:50,910
But every day, she seems a tiny bit less tired.

118
00:04:50,910 --> 00:04:53,990
By loop 400, you know that song by heart.

119
00:04:54,410 --> 00:04:55,290
You've stopped hating it.

120
00:04:55,590 --> 00:04:56,090
You hum it.

121
00:04:56,430 --> 00:05:00,910
And on the very next loop, humming that terrible song changes everything.

122
00:05:01,410 --> 00:05:04,530
On loop 401, the song starts at 9:14,

123
00:05:04,970 --> 00:05:06,890
and someone behind you is humming it too.

124
00:05:07,290 --> 00:05:07,810
It's Leah.

125
00:05:08,270 --> 00:05:09,610
And she knows every word.

126
00:05:10,070 --> 00:05:11,550
You know this song, she says.

127
00:05:12,010 --> 00:05:12,930
It's... catchy.

128
00:05:13,450 --> 00:05:14,170
No, it isn't.

129
00:05:14,430 --> 00:05:14,970
It's terrible.

130
00:05:15,390 --> 00:05:16,230
Nobody knows it.

131
00:05:16,450 --> 00:05:19,750
It came out on a Tuesday three weeks ago, and nobody played it again.

132
00:05:19,750 --> 00:05:20,850
She looks at you.

133
00:05:21,330 --> 00:05:22,410
Except at 9:14.

134
00:05:23,050 --> 00:05:23,730
Every day.

135
00:05:24,210 --> 00:05:25,410
How many loops are you on?

136
00:05:25,810 --> 00:05:26,290
she asks.

137
00:05:26,910 --> 00:05:27,410
401.

138
00:05:28,490 --> 00:05:30,110
I'm on 1,006.

139
00:05:30,730 --> 00:05:34,270
Leah has been stuck in this day 600 loops longer than you.

140
00:05:34,570 --> 00:05:36,110
That's why she knew your coffee order.

141
00:05:36,450 --> 00:05:38,990
She's watched you order it more than a thousand times.

142
00:05:39,410 --> 00:05:40,570
Now there are two of you.

143
00:05:40,710 --> 00:05:42,690
And it's the best 50 days of your life.

144
00:05:43,010 --> 00:05:44,970
You break into the rooftop observatory.

145
00:05:45,170 --> 00:05:48,830
You eat at every restaurant in town, in one day, 50 times.

146
00:05:48,830 --> 00:05:49,930
You teach her guitar.

147
00:05:50,330 --> 00:05:52,210
She teaches you the Japanese you got wrong.

148
00:05:52,530 --> 00:05:54,010
You make a game of the song.

149
00:05:54,370 --> 00:05:56,290
At 9:13, you both count down,

150
00:05:56,490 --> 00:05:58,650
and when it starts at 9:14, you sing it,

151
00:05:59,010 --> 00:06:01,730
loudly and badly, while the whole café stares.

152
00:06:02,150 --> 00:06:04,450
And she tells you about her 600 loops alone.

153
00:06:05,010 --> 00:06:06,230
The loop where she learned to juggle.

154
00:06:06,570 --> 00:06:09,330
The loop where she got lost in the woods and made friends with a deer.

155
00:06:09,790 --> 00:06:13,710
And the 100 loops in a row where she just slept, because she was too sad to get

156
00:06:13,710 --> 00:06:13,950
up.

157
00:06:14,310 --> 00:06:17,010
600 days of the same Tuesday, she says.

158
00:06:17,010 --> 00:06:19,950
You start to forget what it's like when tomorrow is different.

159
00:06:20,410 --> 00:06:21,670
Sam is very confused.

160
00:06:22,170 --> 00:06:25,270
From his side, you and Leah met this morning, and you've been finishing each

161
00:06:25,270 --> 00:06:26,590
other's sentences all day.

162
00:06:27,010 --> 00:06:28,350
Since when are you two a thing?

163
00:06:28,790 --> 00:06:30,290
About 40 days, says Leah.

164
00:06:30,730 --> 00:06:31,830
It's been one day.

165
00:06:32,310 --> 00:06:33,170
Sure, Sam.

166
00:06:33,590 --> 00:06:36,530
But one night on the roof, she tells you why she thinks it's happening.

167
00:06:36,990 --> 00:06:39,910
The first loop, I was supposed to tell someone something important.

168
00:06:40,370 --> 00:06:41,670
Tonight. At the party.

169
00:06:42,170 --> 00:06:42,730
I didn't.

170
00:06:43,050 --> 00:06:44,270
And then the day started again.

171
00:06:44,710 --> 00:06:45,150
Tell who?

172
00:06:45,550 --> 00:06:46,250
She doesn't answer.

173
00:06:46,250 --> 00:06:49,310
You notice something you missed for 400 loops.

174
00:06:49,850 --> 00:06:53,090
Every night at the party, at 11:58, she leaves.

175
00:06:53,590 --> 00:06:54,910
Two minutes before midnight.

176
00:06:55,330 --> 00:06:56,110
Every time.

177
00:06:56,590 --> 00:06:59,530
On loop 450, you don't let her leave.

178
00:06:59,990 --> 00:07:01,770
You always go at 11:58.

179
00:07:02,350 --> 00:07:02,650
Why?

180
00:07:03,130 --> 00:07:07,530
Because the first time, at 11:58, I was going to tell you I liked you.

181
00:07:07,790 --> 00:07:08,970
And I walked out instead.

182
00:07:09,430 --> 00:07:11,290
And I've been too scared to try again,

183
00:07:11,470 --> 00:07:15,350
because if I said it and you didn't feel the same, I'd have to live that answer

184
00:07:15,350 --> 00:07:16,550
over and over.

185
00:07:17,130 --> 00:07:17,410
Forever.

186
00:07:18,110 --> 00:07:19,470
A thousand and six loops.

187
00:07:20,050 --> 00:07:23,570
She's been reliving this day because of the exact same thing you have.

188
00:07:23,970 --> 00:07:24,990
You don't have a speech.

189
00:07:25,410 --> 00:07:27,350
You threw it away 400 loops ago.

190
00:07:27,850 --> 00:07:29,570
I've liked you since freshman chemistry.

191
00:07:30,010 --> 00:07:32,230
You told me my safety goggles were on upside down.

192
00:07:32,570 --> 00:07:33,970
I didn't even say thank you.

193
00:07:34,230 --> 00:07:35,610
I just walked into a cabinet.

194
00:07:36,090 --> 00:07:37,610
I remember the cabinet.

195
00:07:38,170 --> 00:07:39,130
The clock hits midnight.

196
00:07:40,530 --> 00:07:42,050
Then 12:01.

197
00:07:42,050 --> 00:07:43,490
You wake up at 7.

198
00:07:43,870 --> 00:07:44,410
You flinch.

199
00:07:44,870 --> 00:07:45,730
The date has changed.

200
00:07:46,210 --> 00:07:46,630
It's Wednesday.

201
00:07:47,250 --> 00:07:48,070
Your phone buzzes.

202
00:07:48,370 --> 00:07:48,670
It's Leah.

203
00:07:49,170 --> 00:07:50,910
Oat latte, extra hot, no foam?

204
00:07:51,370 --> 00:07:54,270
And at 9:14, the song comes on.

205
00:07:54,690 --> 00:07:55,630
You both groan.

206
00:07:56,030 --> 00:07:57,050
It's still terrible.

207
00:07:57,530 --> 00:07:59,350
Sam still has cereal on his hoodie.

208
00:07:59,890 --> 00:08:01,490
Some things don't need a time loop.

209
00:08:02,010 --> 00:08:05,690
So would you rather be stuck in a time loop with your crush, or never get a

210
00:08:05,690 --> 00:08:06,590
second chance at all?

211
00:08:06,930 --> 00:08:07,650
Tell me in the comments.

